hello and welcome to our first Q&amp;A first  live Q&amp;A here at the Economist my name  is Anna bucks I'm a producer director  with the films department and we are  here to talk about Hong Kong in the  situation in Hong Kong and a lot of you  have already sent in some questions  thank you very much for that and if  you've got any questions over the next  15 minutes or so please do send them in  and I will be asking Robert guest who is  a foreign editor here at the Economist  hello Robert thank you for joining us  today my pleasure and this is a very  important topic the situation in Hong  Kong is one that the Economist has  covered a loss over the last few weeks  we've had many or a couple of front  covers on air why is it such an  important situation well there's two  reasons firstly if you have a territory  of 7.3 million people and a quarter of  them come out onto the streets yet again  over the weekend demanding some say in  how they're governed demanding not to be  put at the mercy of China's opaque and  entirely lawless judicial system partly  lawless judicial system that is that  matters these are people their rights  matter but the second reason why it's of  global consequence is because it tells  you something about China's future  relations with the outside world Hong  Kong is the gateway to China it is the  place where the majority of foreign  direct investment that goes into China  flows through Hong Kong is the place  where foreign companies that want to  have their headquarters that they want  to do business in China will place their  headquarters it's the place where  Chinese companies if they're private and  they're reputable and they they seek  global recognition they will list in  Hong Kong  rather than in the Chinese stock market  so it's absolutely crucial place the  freedoms that it has are a crucial part  of how China has managed to succeed over  the past three decades and it's facing  the question you know is China going to  snuff this out or not and I should just  point out I see that we made a thermal I  made a film last week which you can  watch if you click on me  I on the screen which is about what's at  stake for China but perhaps you could  just go into a bit more detail about the  sort of the dilemma that China is facing  what China faces a huge dilemma here on  the one hand you know the people in  charge in in Beijing or rational they  they know that if they were to send the  army in and crush the the demonstrations  in Hong Kong which they could physically  they could very easily do that you have  a very large army just parked across the  border you have a garrison already in  Hong Kong they could crush them but if  they did that they would destroy the  whole understanding the whole basis of  what's made Hong Kong so so prosperous  and important the feeling that this was  a place where you could be in China you  could be doing business with China but  you would be under different systems a  place where businesses could feel that  the rule of law replied that they  couldn't be just spirited away into some  dark secret dungeon somewhere because  they defended someone powerful within  the party now all those things would be  under threat if China were to crush this  revolt so my guess is that they're  probably not going to but at the same  time you know can they tolerate can the  authoritarian regime in China which  remember has a ruler who has pretty much  declared himself ruler for life can they  tolerate the open and mass expression of  dissent of people demanding a say in who  their rulers are that's not something  they would tolerate on the mainland so  the question is how long are they  prepared to let that last in Hong Kong  and that leads us on to our first  question and a lot of people have been  asking this and if you've got any other  questions that you want to send in  please do and what was the possibility  the protesters might lose spirits and  just sort of decide to call it a day  without any major concessions and  someone else wants to know Khan what is  the endgame here it's a similar question  okay well over the weekend we've just  had by the protestors estimates 1.7  million people came out in the rain on  the streets despite the fear of losing  their jobs despite the fear  of being shot in the eye bright the fear  of being killed that's a quarter of the  population of Hong Kong now the the  police have come up with a lower  estimate than that but there I fear  rather biased and their estimate is  massively contradicted by the the  pictures that we've seen on television  so that suggests that people are  actually passionate about this that they  really care about their future and that  they would you know they want  concessions now can they last four years  that's that's an open question we don't  know and we've got a question from Milan  Prasad who asks are the potestas  rallying behind a leader or is there any  sort of central leadership for their  sort of their struggle can you just tell  us a bit about how the protesters are  organized well there is a loose  coalition of pro-democracy groups and  NGOs sometimes helped by unions and  other groups who are calling for the  demonstrations and are being answered in  very large numbers there isn't one  leader I think if there was just one  leader that would be a very dangerous  position to have so there's a deliberate  dispersal of responsibilities because  you don't want to movement can be  decapitated by removing one person I  mean I'm interested you've covered a lot  of Patera tests over your many years  working journalism how does the Hong  Kong protest compared to other process  that you've covered and do you think  that ultimately they will achieve what  they want well it's a very large protest  to begin with very few places I can  think of in the world where you can get  those kinds of crowds on the street it's  it's been mostly people it hasn't been  entirely peaceful obviously you know the  police have a shot you know beanbag  pellets into people's eyes and have been  some demonstrators including over the  weekend which was mostly peaceful there  were some demonstrators who threw eggs  at a police station and overturned some  rubbish bins but by the standards of  protests this big it has been very  peaceful and it's essential that it  remains so because  if the protesters start you know killing  or injuring people that will give the  authorities a pretext to crack down very  hard indeed the thing that is difficult  it is it is always much easier for a  non-violent protest movement to succeed  if the people they're protesting against  have some conscience so you know  demonstrations against Saddam Hussein  never worked because he just killed  everybody demonstrations again you know  Gandhi's demonstrations against the  British in India ultimately prevailed  because you know with some exceptions  the you know the ruling power was not  prepared to use massive lethal force on  an entire country that big so you know  those those things tend to win with  China it's very hard to say I mean you  know they don't want to have lots of  people dead in the streets but they have  in the past demonstrated a willingness  to do that now in the channel Minh  Square massacre thirty years ago that  was you know that was hotly debated  within the Chinese leadership there were  those who said no we should just let the  demonstrations continue they will burn  themselves out eventually and there were  those who said no we cannot tolerate any  public affront to the authority of the  Communist Party to rule forever without  reference to voters and let's shoot them  now and that was the rule that prevailed  my suspicion is that the Chinese army is  more restrained and more professional  now than it was thirty years ago and  that the leadership is more attuned to  just what a disaster it would be if they  put the army onto the streets of Hong  Kong and started killing people  but you can never rule anything out I  mean a lot of a lot of the comments that  we've had have been about international  response and a lot of people have  mentioned the fact that Hong Kong was a  British colony and have asked will the  UK intervene you know what what is  British responsibility here well I mean  you can argue that Britain has a  historic responsibility but I don't  think it has any power to intervene I  mean British government could make a  statement  I don't know saying  possibly the Chinese government was not  adhering to all the agreements that were  made at the time of the handover in the  1990s but it doesn't realistically have  any any leverage to make the Chinese  government do anything other than what  the Chinese government wants to do this  is this is a crisis that will be  resolved based on the decisions of the  Chinese government and the people of  Hong Kong we just had a comment let's  just come in that says um do you foresee  the u.s. getting more involved in this  affair no I mean it was very clear that  Donald Trump is not interested he has in  fact quite wrongly characterized the  movement as being a riot he's not paying  attention to the details he's interested  in his trade dispute with China which he  views purely through the lens of what's  happening to American manufacturing so  no I don't think that the Americans are  going to get involved now interestingly  within China within the Chinese media  there's this ridiculous conspiracy  theory that is being put out that this  is entirely cooked up by the West that  this is not a spontaneous display by  Hong Kong as of dissatisfaction with the  authoritarian Big Brother next door but  something that has been fermented and  orchestrated by foreign forces meaning  the Americans that's ridiculous I mean  personally they have no reason to want  to do that and secondly they have no  capacity to do that the idea that the  CIA could get two million people onto  the streets of Hong Kong is ridiculous  the frightening thing is as our Jaguar  columnist pointed out recently quite a  lot of people within the Chinese  government appear to believe this and  that's worrying and if you want to read  that you can click on the I now and I  should hopefully give you the link to  that article on The Economist website  and if it's surrett if the UK isn't  gonna do anything in America is not  going to do anything you know if you're  a Hong Kong protester right now who do  you ask for help from who can help you  we know any organization in which card  the countries  is going to come to their aid well no  one I mean they they they need to stick  together they need to stand firm and  they need to be aware that although the  the Chinese government has most of the  power it doesn't hold all of the cards  by being out on the streets and asking  for things they create a set of  incentives whereby it's very much in the  interest of the Chinese government to be  patient to wait to possibly make some  concessions and to hope for this to you  know eventually get quieter but it's not  that Outsiders are going to come to the  rescue of Hong Kongers it's that Hong  Kong is by boldly expressing their  desire for the freedoms that they were  promised may prevail a lot of our  commentators our commenters have been  asking about I've been asking about  Taiwan and they've been asking about  Macau could you just talk a little bit  about how this the situation in Hong  Kong might affect Taiwan is the really  important one so the Chinese government  one of their most important stated goals  is the reunification of Taiwan with the  motherland now the way things stand at  the moment where Taiwan is a rich  democracy and China is a dictatorship  there's no way that that's going to  happen voluntarily people don't can't  get sort of you know 20 million people  and make them voluntarily give up the  right to vote voluntarily give up the  freedom that they currently enjoy from  being arrested in the middle of the  night by an unaccountable States nobody  surrenders that voluntarily the only way  that in the relatively short term you  could imagine a voluntary reunification  ie a reunification without a war would  be if the Hong Kong one country two  systems' idea were seem to be working if  it was seen to be the case that Hong  Kong  having been promised a continuation of  the rule of law and civil liberties for  50 years after the handover if that was  really respected by China so this is  this is the other really big reason why  it would be disastrous from China's  perspective from the perspective of the  Chinese Communist Party to crush the  demonstrations in Hong Kong is that  would make it absolutely impossible to  get a voluntary reunification with  Taiwan and of course a war would be  catastrophic and just to ask you about  Macau we've got a question here from  Wendland SEMA and Macau is another  former colony why does it remain largely  quiet compared to Hong Kong it's much  smaller yes it's not a place I mean Hong  Kong has a sort of vibrant dynamic  mobile outward-facing economy with lots  of people passing through and lots of a  broad understanding among the population  of the benefits of dealing with the  outside world and of the rule of law  Macau is is a special place you know  they they have casinos there but it's  nowhere near as as it's nowhere near as  sophisticated as the places of Hong Kong  but I think we should leave it here  there thank you very much Robert and  thank you for joining me thank you so  much to all your questions as well and  this is the first in a series of live  YouTube Q&amp;A that when we're running if  you click on the bell then you can  subscribe and you will be able to let  you know when the next one is coming up  and that's going to be your own  reforestation and climate change we'll  be asking countries save the planet if  you want to read more about what The  Economist has written on Hong Kong and 